<Base title="About Lewisburg">
  <p class="eyebrow">The place</p>
  <h1>About Lewisburg</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    One of the oldest settlements on the north shore, and still one of the quietest.
  </p>

  <div class="article-body">
    <p>
      Lewisburg lies along the north shore of Lake Pontchartrain in Mandeville, just west of the
      Causeway. It is a small, largely residential neighborhood of live oaks and Spanish moss,
      bounded by the lake to the south and Bayou Chinchuba to the west.
    </p>

    <h2>The Seven Sisters Oak</h2>
    <p>
      The neighborhood's best-known resident is a tree. The Seven Sisters Oak is the largest certified
      live oak in North America — roughly 39 feet in circumference, with a crown spreading more than
      130 feet, and an estimated age of about 1,000 years. It has been the reigning president of the
      Live Oak Society for decades.
    </p>
    <p>
      The name comes from the trunk's habit of appearing to split into several trunks near the base.
      The tree stands on private property, and neighbors ask that visitors admire it from the street.
    </p>

    <h2>Sarah Sale's tombstone</h2>
    <p>
      A single marked grave sits within the neighborhood, a reminder that Lewisburg was a working
      lakefront community long before it was a subdivision. The stone is one of the few visible traces
      of the settlement's earliest decades.
    </p>

    <h2>The association</h2>
    <p>
      The Lewisburg Neighborhood Association exists to keep residents informed, to organize the things
      that are better done together — the picnic, the cleanups — and to speak with one voice when
      decisions get made about this stretch of shoreline.
    </p>

